  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fair weather for most areas except the Cape and\nIslands Sunday and Monday. A Rip Current Statement has been \nissued for Cape Cod tonight into Sunday.\n\nAnother mostly clear day Sunday as weak high pressure maintains\ncontrol. Height falls ahead of an approaching trough and a low \npressure system passing well offshore brings moisture and clouds\nto the Cape and Islands Sunday afternoon. Model soundings \nindicate a shallow layer of moisture at the surface with dry air\nabove. It is more likely that this layer will be too shallow to\nsupport any showers/drizzle. A worst case would be some patchy \ndrizzle for the outer Cape/Islands. Aside from the clouds in \nsome areas, it will be a mainly dry day with a northeasterly \nbreeze for the eastern coastal areas. Temperatures likely range \nin the low to mid 80s for the interior and mid to upper 70s in \nthe eastern and coastal areas. \n\nA Rip Current Statement has been issued for the Cape as \neasterly winds and prolonged swells leads to a moderate to high \nchance for rip currents. While the highest risk exists along the\nCape, an elevated chance of rip currents spans the south and \neast coastline with the greatest risk for the east-facing \nbeaches along the Cape and Islands and portions of the east \ncoast of MA.\n\nSlightly warmer conditions across the interior on Monday with \n925 mb temps warming to +20-23C translating to max temps in the \nmid and upper 80s. A deeper shortwave trough moves overhead, and\nwith associated mid-level cooling should introduce increasing \ncloud cover by the afternoon. Guidance shows a 20-40% chance of \nshowers across western portions of the CWA as forcing and \ninstability build.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures on the lower end of seasonable as \nperiodic chances for showers and thunderstorms return Mon-Wed \n(possibly into Thurs). Monitoring for increasing chances of \nlocalized heavy rainfall and potentially flash flooding.\n\nEnsemble guidance has started to come into better consensus \nthat a low pressure system will traverse through the Great Lakes\nMonday/Tuesday and into New England around Wednesday. This \nsystem will likely be accompanied by several embedded shortwaves\nand a mid- level trough, bringing chances for a few rounds of \nshowers, and possibly thunderstorms, Monday through Wednesday...\npossibly lingering into Thursday. There continues to be a \nstrong signal for PWATs 120-170 percent of normal to advect into\nthe region by Tuesday, with deterministic guidance on the \nextreme high end of that range. Some guidance is also honing in \non some potential for the low to slow on approach to New England\nas well as slower mid-level wind speeds, which would create an \nenvironment for training storms. Combined with the enhanced \nforcing of the low, the higher PWATs and potential for training,\nlocalized heavy rainfall and potentially flash flooding are \nbecoming something to watch for. Unfortunately, there still \nremains a large amount of uncertainty in the guidance regarding \nwhere exactly the low could track, with some models indicating \nthe low moving further south near NYC and some further north \nthrough northern MA. Depending on where exactly the low tracks, \nit's possible the threat ends up west/south of the region or \nconfined to western portions of southern New England.\n\nOtherwise, high temperatures are expected to remain on the \nlower end of seasonable, even dipping to slightly below normal \nat times given 850mb temperatures hovering around +12C to +14C \nthrough mid-week.",
